<problem>
<!-- <title>Indefinite integral &#x2D; exponential</title> -->

<setup>

<selectFromSequence name="a" from="-10" to="10" numToSelect="2" withReplacement="true" exclude="0" />
<function name="g" variables="t" simplify="numbers" symbolic="true">$a[1] *e^($a[2] *t)</function>
<function name="g_simple" variables="t" simplify="numbers" symbolic="true">e^($a[2] *t)</function>
<function name="expAntiderivative" variables="t" simplify="numbers" symbolic="true">($a[1]/$a[2])*e^($a[2] *t)</function>
<math name="intgeval" simplify="numbers">$$expAntiderivative(t)</math>
<math name="intgeval_C" simplify="numbersPreserveOrder">$intgeval +C</math>
</setup>

<p>  
Evaluate the following integral:</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle\int $g dt =</m>
<answer>
  <award symbolicEquality="true" simplifyOnCompare="true">$intgeval_C </award>
  <award credit="0.67" name="award_noC" symbolicEquality="true" simplifyOnCompare="true">$intgeval</award>
</answer>
</p>
  
<feedback condition="$award_noC">Don't forget the constant of integration.</feedback>
<hint>Do not round or use a decimal approximation.  Use exact, simplified fractions in your response.</hint>



<solution>
<p>We first use the constant product rules for integration:</p><p><m>\displaystyle \int $g dt = $a[1] \int $g_simple dt</m>
</p>

<p>We then find the antiderivative: </p>
<p><m>\displaystyle $a[1] \int $g_simple dt = $intgeval_C</m></p>

</solution>
</problem>


